cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "Please cite this repository with the metadata below."
title: "Measured steps toward a logic-based language model with Tsetlin Machines"
abstract: >-
  This repository holds the code, the registrations and the results pages of
  nine studies (19 Sep to 29 Oct 2026) that take measured steps toward a
  language model built from Tsetlin Machine clauses; run folders, result
  files and datasets are not distributed. The first study built a
  token-level Tsetlin Machine language model on a sparse event-driven engine
  and compared it with a Kneser-Ney 5-gram model on the same tokens: its
  reproducibility hypotheses were met at the scale tested, and its main
  accuracy goal was missed on the one seed that was run, which triggered the
  registered kill criterion. The second study tested the hierarchical
  Tsetlin Machine procedure of an unpublished project proposal on artificial
  data, two sentence families, news topics and Wikipedia paragraph pairs and
  ended mostly negative: its single final test is below word overlap on both
  Wikipedia tasks. The third study is preparation for a word-level clause
  language model: count baselines and one evaluator ran without a Tsetlin
  Machine, the reproduction of an existing Tsetlin Machine router met its
  first gate only under a post hoc amendment, and the registered test of its
  specificity s that followed is negative. The fourth study tested the
  Tsetlin Machine as the decision layer over language encoders, from intent
  routing to agents that choose the next action in dialogues: a single
  Tsetlin Machine router trails logistic regression on the same embedding,
  memory literals lift the next-action accuracy on ABCD by 18.65 points, and,
  with the same tuning, an agent with no neural network at any stage is not
  worse, within the registered margin, than an agent that runs a
  transformer encoder per decision on three dialogue test splits, two of
  which had been read many times before, while the registered test of
  superiority in that comparison, on the third split, is not met. The fifth study holds the first steps of a
  count-based language model with places where a Tsetlin Machine must beat a
  twin that only counts; its registered test has finished its runs and has
  no verdict yet.
authors:
